A||||12||Ozzy||'88 Duke ticking noise?||||5||
Z||000000||Ozzy||02-07-2000||12:09 AM||||Hi People.<P>Well, I just got my last problem resolved and now seem to have another smaller(?) one to deal with.<P>I installed new plugs, wires, O2 sensor and did some small maintenance type stuff to my duke. Nothing major, just redid some vacuum lines, installed some hose clamps and cleaned up some grounds. Anyway, I start it up and I hear this ticking noise that sounds like a hydraulic lifter. I thought it may have been a plug not seated properly due to muck and a lack of room to locate the plug threads but I removed each in turn and checked.<P>Are the lifters hydraulic in this engine? ('88 coupe) And do they just 'go' like that?<P>If none of the above, what could it be? Suggestions anyone?||||reg||5
Z||000001||JetroGT||02-07-2000||12:28 AM||||It could be your cam gear going bad. If it is the cam gear, replace it ASAP. GM used that fiber garbage gear to make the car a little more quiet. My ex "not wife thank God!" had the same problem. she did not listen to me. Then while driving back from a long trip all of the teeth finally broke off. She blew the moter and ended up buying some hundyi. I just laughed.||||reg||1
Z||000002||Phaeton||02-07-2000||05:07 AM||||If an air leak is in the correct spot to go supersonic it will 'crack' with a very metallic sound, exhaust forward of the converter is prone to this, the closer to the head the more metallic the sound.  Anything bumped or tightened around pipe when doing 02 sensor?  Lifters are hydraulic, maybe your ear was distracted earlier and didn't notice this particular noise, which could be a fuel injector cycling. Nurse! Stethyscope! Stat!||||reg||1
Z||000003||theogre||02-07-2000||09:37 AM||||check the exhaust manifold. It's prone to fine cracking that can make a ticking noise depending on just where it cracked. You may have to take it off and check the engine side. ||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ozzy||02-07-2000||01:23 PM||||Definatley not the timing gear. The noise is coming from the other end of the engine. The noise also was not there when I pulled the vehichle in to the garage to work on it. Seems kinda odd that the manifold would crack like that. I did not have to exert any real force on the O2 sensor so I don't believe that would have disturbed anything. But who's to say...not the first time this car has had 'unexpected' problems.<P>Back to the lifter issue...Don't they generally start to gradually go bad? I will pull the exhaust manifold off if I have to but just want to be confident that is the problem first. I'm sure it's a great big pain in the A** to do.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000005||Phaeton||02-08-2000||03:01 AM||||Didn't tick when you took it in, now it ticks.  What ARE the odds of a lifter going flat right then?  OK, it's a Fiero so odds are fifty fifty.  But vacuum lines redone and O2 sensor changed meant open channels to intake and exhaust.  Checking every millimeter of vacuum lines and exhaust with a length of rubber tubing held to your ear is still easier than removing hard parts from the engine.  And if the Fiero still needs to be taken apart...look at all the justifiable fondling that took place. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000007||Patrick||02-08-2000||03:09 AM||||<BR><B>Ozzy</B>, two things...<P>A hydraulic lifter can go just like that if it has a buildup of crud in it and the engine has been revved up high. The lifter can stick in a collapsed position.<P>Are you sure you aren't hearing the "snap" of a spark bridging a gap. If it was shorting to ground somewhere, the engine would be kinda rough, but it's possible for the spark to jump a gap and still make it to the combustion chamber. This could happen for example if one or more of the spark plug wires weren't pushed onto the plugs or into the distributor cap far enough.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Ozzy||02-08-2000||06:40 PM||||Hey Guys...<P>Yeah I guess it's possible that there may be a spark. I thought I pushed those new wires into place pretty convincingly but I will check.<P>It is definatley a metalic sounding tick. I would not have thought that a leaking vacuum line would sound like that??? I will definatley take your advice though and check it over again.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000009||86SE||02-08-2000||10:30 PM||||ITS A BOMB!!!!!!!!  AHHHHHH!!!!!<P>my v-6 ticks, it has a cracked (firewall side) exhaust manifold  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif">||||reg||11
Z||000010||WKDFIRO||02-08-2000||10:35 PM||||Looking at the exhaust manifold of a 4 and a 6 cyl makes me wonder which high school metal shop got the contract for making manifolds for GM. They are the most ridiculous looking things I have seen. Yes they crack. But sometimes the crack can be clever, hiding down under looking like a weld line but they do generally do the same thing. The louder and softer depending on the temp of the engine. <P>The other fun thing it could be is a loose manifold bolt. The pulses make the same noise and drive mechanics crazy! Make sure those bolts are tight and eliminate another possibilty!<P>Now to really fry your cookie. In my 87 4 cyl I did have a for real lifter tap. I was deflated and disgusted but I couldn't be surprised with 150k on it. I called Keith from Fiero Motorsports and he told me to do a weird thing. He said to drive somewhere far on the freeway. If it was a collapsed lifter sometimes if given enough time at a constant speed it would rehydrate. <P>As the odometer tripped 230 miles (About the 4th lap from LA to Riverside) the tap went away...never came back for the next 30k miles. <BR>T<BR>he next tap I heard was from the Ford full size P/U slamming into the back of me at 40 mph.....but thats another story.....||||reg||12
Z||000011||Ozzy||02-09-2000||10:36 AM||||(lol) Dat's the kinda tap I wanna avoid!!!<P>All makes sense though. BTW, albeit a little late, I have headers on this engine and not the stock manifold. Can't tell you what make they are but definatly header pipes. <P>The other thing I had suggested to me was to put a dixie cup fulla Marvell's Mystery oil in the engine oil. This would help to remove any varnish type buildup without damage to the engine.(Assuming it is a lifter.)Suggested to me by a mechanic.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
Z||000012||kidzwheelz||02-09-2000||01:44 PM||||  Ozzy, the iron duke is rather notorious for ticking sounds in the valve train, hydraulic lifters or not. But, if your car just started ticking after you have worked on it, my bet is an exhaust leak. It doesn't take a whole lot of pressure to cause this when you lean on or against the exhaust manifold. I don't think you will find a cracked manifold, and I don't believe it's a collapsed lifter. I could be wrong, this is a very quirky little car, but I think the safe bet is a simple leak. $5.99 for a new gasket, and spend the rest of your time and money on beer!||||reg||4
Z||000013||RossT||02-10-2000||11:58 PM||||Ozzy, here in the northland we use a product called  (SEAFOAM). It is somewhat like the Marvel product. In addition to removing varnish and carbon build up, it helps remove the moisture sludge, that doesn't burn of, cause it stays to fricken cold out here.  It stays so cold, that manytimes the thermostat will not even open. I am normally against additives of anykind, but this product is good stuff. Its the only additve I use from time to time.||||reg||1
